# ERP Unite — Executive Brief

**For:** HSO executive committee
**From:** Solution architecture
**Date:** May 2026
**Re:** ERP Unite — productized insurance brokerage finance platform inherited from OneDigital

## Executive Summary

ERP Unite is a finance platform purpose-built for insurance brokerages. It handles statement ingestion with AI-driven mapping, automated cash application, journal generation to Accounting Hub, month-end accruals, collections, and producer compensation — all on one connected data model. HSO has inherited the codebase and is in Phase 1 of deploying it to HSO infrastructure as a productized offering.

This brief covers what the platform is, what HSO inherits, and what the next ninety days require to take it to market.

## What ERP Unite Is

Insurance brokerages have three intertwined finance problems: cash from carriers does not apply itself, commissions get calculated in spreadsheets, and month-end close consumes days of staff time per cycle. ERP Unite addresses all three on a single platform, with four distinct applications sharing one domain model.

The platform consists of:

- **Cash Hub** — Statement ingestion, cash application matching, journal generation, accruals, collections, research cases.
- **Compensation Hub** — Compensation actuals calculation, producer compensation rules, split intake with versioning, payable transaction integration.
- **Producer Portal** — External-facing self-service for producers and brokers, dual-OIDC authentication for HSO and customer-tenant users.
- **Integration Hub** — Six Azure Function apps providing inbound and outbound bridges to Accounting Hub, FinOps / Dynamics 365, Workday, Mulesoft, Databricks, Microsoft Entra ID, and Google Gemini.

## What HSO Inherits

The asset is mature. This is not a science project being absorbed into the portfolio. It is a working product against a real production database.

Concrete inventory:

- Four .NET 10 Blazor Server applications, deployment-ready
- Six Azure Function applications providing approximately 90 integration touchpoints
- More than 70 domain entities with full Entity Framework Core configurations and migration history
- More than 200 CQRS command and query handlers wired across the four applications
- More than 90 specialized repositories implementing a dual-constructor pattern that supports both dependency-injection-driven access and transactional unit-of-work access
- A complete authorization model with global, regional, and hierarchy-based permission scopes
- AI integration via Google Gemini for statement template authoring and column extraction, with audit-traceable inference

The architecture follows strict layering and CQRS without MediatR. Reads use `IDbContextFactory` with short-lived contexts and projection. Writes use a Unit of Work pattern with explicit transaction boundaries. The boundaries are enforced by code review and a published architecture guide, not by reflection.

## The Differentiated Capability — AI-Driven Mapping Studio

The clearest competitive moat in ERP Unite is the Mapping Studio. Brokerages receive commission statements from hundreds of carriers, each in a different format. Today, an analyst opens each statement and re-keys it into the general ledger.

Mapping Studio replaces that workflow:

- The user drops a carrier statement file into the system.
- Google Gemini analyzes the file structure and proposes column mappings: which column holds the customer name, which holds the gross commission amount, which holds the policy number.
- The user reviews and accepts the proposed mappings; the template is now reusable.
- For PDF statements, region-select OCR pulls structured data from embedded tables.
- Value mappings (the carrier code that means "premium" on this statement) are also AI-suggested.
- A natural-language prompt can be translated to executable column extraction script.

The result is that onboarding a new carrier statement format, which traditionally takes two days of analyst work, drops to minutes.

## The Productization Path

The productization plan is documented in `docs/SPEC-productization.md` and divided into four phases.

**Phase 1 — Deploy to HSO cloud.** Currently in progress. The CashHub and Compensation Hub web applications are being deployed to `hsouscloud`. The database is being restored from a OneDigital `bacpac`. PowerShell deployment scripts exist (`deploy-cashhub.ps1`, `deploy-comphub.ps1`). The Phase 1 acceptance criterion is single-sign-on landing-page render against the freshly deployed environment.

**Phase 2 — Source-control move to HSO Azure DevOps.** Begins after Phase 1 verification. The HSO repository will mirror OneDigital's main branch on a long-lived `upstream/main` branch with one-way pull only. The HSO main branch will become the productization branch. Only the two Blazor web app pipelines port in this phase; function app pipelines remain in scope for later.

**Phase 3 — Productize.** Gated on a management alignment meeting that locks the product name (currently a placeholder), the productization scope, and the AI roadmap. Productization itself includes de-branding (replacing every reference to "OneDigital" in code, configuration, comments, and assets), parameterizing OneDigital-specific business logic, possibly adding new AI features, and namespace renames.

**Phase 4 — PII Scrub.** Parallel to Phase 3. Discovery-grade today. Requires column-level inventory of personally identifying information across the schema and per-column transformation strategies.

## What HSO Should Decide Next

Three decisions are blocking forward progress.

1. **Product name.** The codebase calls it "Unite" by namespace convention. The repository is named `HSOInsuranceBrokerAdv`. Neither is a market-facing brand. Marketing and management need to lock a product name before Phase 3 can begin in earnest, because that name appears in every UI string, image asset, and namespace.

2. **AI feature wishlist.** The Mapping Studio is the existing AI capability. Plausible additions include smart cash-match suggestions, anomaly detection on accrual variance, AI-assisted research case routing, and copilots for the back office. Each potential AI feature requires scoping; the HSO AI strategy needs to lock priorities.

3. **Sales motion.** ERP Unite competes neither with the agency management systems brokerages already run (BenefitMall, AgencyBloc, etc.) nor with generic ERPs (NetSuite, Dynamics 365). It sits as the missing finance layer between them. The sales motion — direct, channel, embedded in a larger HSO insurance engagement — needs an owner.

## Strategic Position

ERP Unite is a meaningful portfolio addition for HSO. It is adjacent to the Dynamics 365 Finance practice without competing with it; the platform integrates with D365 patterns deeply and would benefit from HSO's existing D365 implementation capability on customer engagements. It is adjacent to the Power Platform practice; the Mapping Studio's AI integration patterns are themselves a reference architecture for AI-augmented finance workflows. And it positions HSO as a credible product vendor in the insurance vertical, not only a services partner.

The platform is real. The code is mature. The integrations are wired. The path to a sellable HSO product is measured in weeks, not years.

## Recommendation

1. Convene the management alignment meeting in the next two weeks. Lock the product name, the in-scope productization items, and the AI feature roadmap.
2. Authorize Phase 2 (DevOps move) immediately upon Phase 1 acceptance.
3. Identify the sales motion owner before Phase 3 begins, so that productization can be guided by go-to-market needs rather than retrofit them after.

The artifacts supporting this brief — feature map, architecture guide, productization specification, and audience-specific learning packs for sales, functional consultants, technical architects, and enterprise architects — are in `docs/` and `docs/learning-packs/`.

